  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Weak troughing sliding through the region this \nafternoon has kept a narrow corridor of mid-lvl moisture stretching \nfrom northern Nebraska into western Minnesota. This moisture, \nconfined within an area where H850 mb flow 30-40 knots, may \neventually lead to isolated high based showers or thunderstorms \nlater this afternoon. Confidence on development remains low, and \nwill keep PoPs below 20% ATTM. Elsewhere, southerly winds are \nincreasing, finally mixing out low-stratus and fog. High \ntemperatures climb into the upper 80s west of the James River to the \nlower 80s further east. \n\nTONIGHT: Two concerns in the overnight period. First of all, \nsoutherly winds should continue to transport low-lvl moisture \nnorthward, pushing dew points into the mid-60s.  Should winds \nweaken sufficiently and temperatures drops not be overly \nimpacted by widespread wildfire smoke aloft, then areas of fog \nmay once again develop along and south of I-90. HREF \nprobabilities focus a 70% probability of <1 mile visibility \nalong and southeast of a line from Yankton to Windom. However, \nif the second concern we'll discuss next takes place, then those\nfog probability may drop significantly. \n\nThat second concern is a risk for elevated convection by daybreak. \nThe pesky quasi-northwest flow has the potential to bring subtle mid-\nlvl vorticity into the area by daybreak. Guidance is all over \nthe place, which is typical in this setup, suggesting either \nminimal to no convection, or fairly widespread storm \ndevelopment. From a parameter perspective incoming CAA AOA \n850:700 mb will create a steepened mid-lvl lapse rate, but also \npush MUCAPE over 2000 J/KG. Shear remains a bit marginal AOA 20 \nknots, suggesting convection may stay a bit less organized. \nHowever analog soundings indicate potential for golf ball hail \nwith freezing levels near 11K ft AGL, with some modest mid-lvl \ndry air also suggesting a few stronger wind gusts that may make \nit through the stable boundary layer. If this threat develops \nstorms would push southeast with the mean flow and exit late \nmorning.\n\nFRIDAY:  The initial morning convection will precede a cold front \nthat will push southeast during the day. Temperatures behind this \nfront will not be all that cool, with highs again climbing into the \nlower to middle 80s. The rest of the day and evening will be quiet. \nAfter midnight however, some guidance is showing another wave moving \nthrough central SD and into NE.  Should this wave develop, there may \nbe sufficient convergence on the LLJ to induce scattered \nconvection in central and south central South Dakota after 3pm. \nIsolated hail will be the most likely risk with these storms. \n\nSATURDAY/SUNDAY:  There remains quite a bit of uncertainty heading \ninto the upcoming weekend given the synoptic flow pattern. \nSaturday morning convection should continue to push away from \nthe CWA in the morning hours, which would suggest stabilization \nin the Tri-State area for the rest of the day. However the \nnocturnal ignition of the LLJ would pose some risk for renewed \nelevated storms into Sunday morning south of I-90. There seems \nto be a bit more agreement by late Sunday into Monday as a \nstronger wave moves through the Dakotas late in the evening. \nDepending on the degree of instability in the region, this could\nserve as a reasonable setup for a nocturnal MCS given the \nintensifying 850mb southerly flow and increased mid-lvl \nwesterlies. Continue to monitor severe weather outlook risks \ninto Monday morning. \n\nMONDAY-WEDNESDAY: There will really be no change in the forecast \ninto the middle of the week as the region remains under slight \nnorthwesterly flow aloft.  This flow will keep a persistent risk for \ndaily convection, though with extremely low confidence and \npredictability until a day or so before. Temperatures may fall \nback slightly into the lower to middle 80s near the seasonal \nnormals.",
